  <h2>Constitutional Law — Detailed Notes</h2>
  <div class="notes-body"><br>CONSTITUTIONAL LAW – I<br><br>UNIT I — Meaning and Concept of Constitution and Constitutionalism<br><br>1. Constitution — Meaning<br><br>A Constitution is the fundamental and supreme law of a country which establishes:<br><br>• Structure of government<br>• Powers and functions of governmental organs<br>• Relationship between different organs<br>• Distribution of powers between Union and States<br>• Rights and freedoms of individuals<br>• Limitations on governmental power<br><br>The Indian Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949 and came into force on 26 January 1950.<br><br>Functions of a Constitution<br><br>1. Establishes government<br>   Creates Parliament, Executive and Judiciary.<br><br>2. Distributes powers<br>   Especially between Union and State governments.<br><br>3. Limits governmental power<br>   Fundamental Rights restrict arbitrary State action.<br><br>4. Protects individual liberty<br>   Articles 14–32 provide constitutional protection.<br><br>5. Establishes rule of law<br>   Government itself is subject to law.<br><br>6. Provides mechanisms for constitutional change<br>   Article 368 provides amendment procedure.<br><br><br>2. Constitutionalism<br><br>Constitutionalism means the principle that governmental power must be limited and exercised according to constitutional rules.<br><br>A country may have a Constitution but still lack genuine constitutionalism.<br><br>Constitution vs Constitutionalism<br><br>Constitution<br><br>• Fundamental legal document<br>• Creates government<br>• Contains powers<br>• Can exist formally<br>• Example: Indian Constitution<br><br>Constitutionalism<br><br>• Political/legal philosophy<br>• Limits government<br>• Controls exercise of powers<br>• Requires respect for constitutional limitations<br>• Example: Rule of law, separation of powers, judicial review<br><br>Elements of Constitutionalism<br><br>• Rule of law<br>• Limited government<br>• Separation of powers<br>• Judicial review<br>• Fundamental Rights<br>• Independent judiciary<br>• Democratic government<br>• Accountability<br>• Constitutional supremacy<br><br><br>3. Classification of Constitutions<br><br>A. Written and Unwritten<br><br>Written Constitution<br><br>• Rules are substantially contained in a written document.<br>• Example: India, USA.<br><br>Unwritten Constitution<br><br>• Constitutional rules arise from statutes, conventions, judicial decisions and customs.<br>• Example: United Kingdom.<br><br>India has a written and detailed Constitution.<br><br><br>B. Rigid and Flexible<br><br>Rigid Constitution<br><br>• Difficult amendment procedure.<br>• Example: USA.<br><br>Flexible Constitution<br><br>• Can be amended comparatively easily.<br>• Traditional example: UK.<br><br>The Indian Constitution is partly rigid and partly flexible.<br><br>For example:<br><br>• Some provisions can be amended by simple majority.<br>• Some require special majority.<br>• Some require special majority + ratification by at least half of the States.<br><br><br>C. Federal and Unitary<br><br>Federal Constitution<br><br>• Powers constitutionally divided between Union and States.<br>• Written Constitution<br>• Supremacy of Constitution<br>• Independent judiciary<br>• Usually bicameral legislature<br><br>Unitary Constitution<br><br>• Central government possesses predominant authority.<br><br>India has a federal structure with strong unitary features.<br><br><br>4. Sources and Framing of the Indian Constitution<br><br>Major Sources<br><br>The Constitution borrowed several features from other constitutional systems.<br><br>Country — Feature borrowed<br><br>UK — Parliamentary government, Rule of Law, Cabinet system<br>USA — Fundamental Rights, Judicial Review, Independence of Judiciary<br>Ireland — Directive Principles of State Policy<br>Canada — Federation with strong Centre, Residuary powers<br>Australia — Concurrent List, Freedom of trade and commerce<br>Germany — Emergency provisions<br>USSR/Russia — Fundamental Duties<br>South Africa — Amendment procedure<br>Japan — Procedure established by law<br><br>Government of India Act, 1935<br><br>It was one of the most important sources.<br><br>Features influenced by it included:<br><br>• Federal structure<br>• Office of Governor<br>• Public Service Commissions<br>• Emergency provisions<br>• Administrative framework<br>• Distribution of legislative powers<br><br><br>5. Constituent Assembly<br><br>The Constitution was framed by the Constituent Assembly.<br><br>Important personalities<br><br>• Dr. B.R. Ambedkar — Chairman, Drafting Committee<br>• Dr. Rajendra Prasad — President of Constituent Assembly<br>• Jawaharlal Nehru — introduced Objectives Resolution<br>• Sardar Patel — important role in integration and constitutional arrangements<br>• B.N. Rau — Constitutional Adviser<br><br>Drafting Committee<br><br>Chairman: Dr. B.R. Ambedkar<br><br>The Constitution was finally adopted on:<br><br>26 November 1949<br><br>It came into force on:<br><br>26 January 1950.<br><br><br>6. Preamble<br><br>The Preamble describes the basic philosophy and objectives of the Constitution.<br><br>It begins:<br><br>“We, the People of India…”<br><br>This establishes the principle of popular sovereignty.<br><br>Important words<br><br>India is described as:<br><br>• Sovereign<br>• Socialist<br>• Secular<br>• Democratic<br>• Republic<br><br>The objectives are:<br><br>Justice<br><br>• Social<br>• Economic<br>• Political<br><br>Liberty<br><br>• Thought<br>• Expression<br>• Belief<br>• Faith<br>• Worship<br><br>Equality<br><br>• Status<br>• Opportunity<br><br>Fraternity<br><br>• Dignity of individual<br>• Unity and integrity of nation<br><br>42nd Constitutional Amendment, 1976<br><br>Added:<br><br>• Socialist<br>• Secular<br>• Integrity<br><br><br>Is Preamble part of Constitution?<br><br>Kesavananda Bharati v State of Kerala (1973)<br><br>The Supreme Court held that:<br><br>• Preamble is part of the Constitution.<br>• It can be used to interpret constitutional provisions.<br>• It is subject to the basic structure doctrine.<br><br>Berubari Union Reference (1960)<br><br>Initially, the Supreme Court had taken the view that the Preamble was not a source of substantive power.<br><br>This position was subsequently changed in Kesavananda Bharati.<br><br><br>7. Salient Features of Indian Constitution<br><br>1. Written Constitution<br><br>India has one of the world's most detailed written constitutions.<br><br>2. Length and Detail<br><br>It contains extensive provisions dealing with:<br><br>• Government<br>• Rights<br>• Federalism<br>• Elections<br>• Emergency<br>• Judiciary<br>• Local government<br>• Services<br><br>3. Sovereignty of Constitution<br><br>All governmental organs derive their authority from the Constitution.<br><br>4. Parliamentary Government<br><br>The executive is responsible to the legislature.<br><br>5. Federal Structure<br><br>Powers are divided between Union and States.<br><br>6. Strong Centre<br><br>The Union has considerable powers, particularly during emergencies.<br><br>7. Fundamental Rights<br><br>Part III guarantees enforceable fundamental rights.<br><br>8. Directive Principles<br><br>Part IV contains socio-economic objectives for governance.<br><br>9. Fundamental Duties<br><br>Article 51A contains duties of citizens.<br><br>10. Independent Judiciary<br><br>The Supreme Court and High Courts exercise judicial review.<br><br>11. Judicial Review<br><br>Courts can invalidate unconstitutional laws and executive actions.<br><br>12. Universal Adult Franchise<br><br>Citizens meeting the constitutional voting requirements participate in elections.<br><br>13. Single Citizenship<br><br>India generally provides for single citizenship.<br><br>14. Secular State<br><br>The State does not establish an official religion and guarantees religious freedom.<br><br><br>8. Is Indian Constitution Federal?<br><br>India is generally described as:<br><br>“Federal in structure but with strong unitary features.”<br><br>Federal features<br><br>• Written Constitution<br>• Supremacy of Constitution<br>• Division of powers<br>• Independent judiciary<br>• Bicameral Parliament<br>• Constitutional supremacy<br>• Rigid amendment procedure for certain provisions<br><br>Unitary features<br><br>• Strong Union<br>• Single Constitution<br>• Single citizenship<br>• Integrated judiciary<br>• Governors appointed by Union<br>• Parliament's power regarding States in certain circumstances<br>• Emergency provisions<br>• Residuary legislative power with Union<br><br>Important Case<br><br>S.R. Bommai v Union of India (1994)<br><br>The Supreme Court recognised federalism as an important/basic feature of the Constitution.<br><br>Exam conclusion<br><br>India cannot be classified as purely federal or purely unitary. It is better described as a federal system with a strong centralising tendency.<br><br><br>UNIT II — CONSTITUTIONAL ORGANS OF GOVERNMENT<br><br>A. PARLIAMENT<br><br>1. Composition of Parliament<br><br>Article 79 provides that Parliament consists of:<br><br>1. President<br>2. Rajya Sabha<br>3. Lok Sabha<br><br><br>Lok Sabha<br><br>Also called the House of the People.<br><br>Members are directly elected by citizens.<br><br>Major features<br><br>• Direct election<br>• Five-year normal term<br>• Can be dissolved<br>• Money Bills originate only in Lok Sabha<br>• Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to Lok Sabha<br><br><br>Rajya Sabha<br><br>Also called the Council of States.<br><br>Features<br><br>• Permanent House<br>• Not subject to dissolution<br>• One-third members retire every two years<br>• Represents States and Union Territories<br>• Vice-President is ex officio Chairman<br><br><br>2. Parliamentary Sovereignty<br><br>The Indian Parliament is not absolutely sovereign like the traditional British Parliament.<br><br>Parliament is subject to:<br><br>• Constitution<br>• Fundamental Rights<br>• Judicial review<br>• Federal limitations<br>• Basic structure doctrine<br><br>Indian position<br><br>Constitutional supremacy &gt; Parliamentary supremacy<br><br>Parliament can make laws, but cannot destroy the basic structure of the Constitution.<br><br><br>3. Parliamentary Privileges<br><br>Parliamentary privileges are special rights and immunities enjoyed by Parliament and its members to enable them to perform their functions effectively.<br><br>Article 105<br><br>Deals with privileges of Parliament.<br><br>Article 194<br><br>Deals with privileges of State Legislatures.<br><br>Important privileges<br><br>• Freedom of speech in Parliament<br>• Immunity for certain parliamentary proceedings<br>• Right to regulate internal proceedings<br>• Power to punish for contempt/breach of privilege<br><br>Important Case<br><br>Searchlight Case — M.S.M. Sharma v Sri Krishna Sinha<br><br>The Court considered the relationship between parliamentary privilege and fundamental rights.<br><br>Raja Ram Pal v Speaker, Lok Sabha (2007)<br><br>The Supreme Court recognised that parliamentary proceedings are subject to limited judicial review, particularly where constitutional limitations are violated.<br><br><br>B. EXECUTIVE<br><br>President of India<br><br>Articles 52–62 primarily deal with the President.<br><br>Constitutional position<br><br>The President is the constitutional/formal head of the Union Executive.<br><br>Article 53 vests executive power of the Union in the President.<br><br>However, under Article 74, the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers.<br><br><br>Powers of President<br><br>1. Executive powers<br><br>• Appointment of Prime Minister<br>• Appointment of ministers<br>• Governors<br>• Attorney General<br>• CAG<br>• Election Commissioners and other constitutional authorities as provided by law<br><br>2. Legislative powers<br><br>• Summoning Parliament<br>• Proroguing Parliament<br>• Dissolving Lok Sabha<br>• Addressing Parliament<br>• Assent to Bills<br>• Ordinance-making power under Article 123<br><br>3. Financial powers<br><br>• Money Bill requires prior recommendation<br>• Budget presented in President's name<br>• Certain financial Bills require presidential recommendation<br><br>4. Judicial powers<br><br>Article 72:<br><br>President can grant:<br><br>• Pardon<br>• Reprieve<br>• Respite<br>• Remission<br>• Commutation<br><br><br>Governor<br><br>Articles 153–162 primarily deal with Governor.<br><br>The Governor is the constitutional head of a State.<br><br>Powers<br><br>• Executive<br>• Legislative<br>• Financial<br>• Judicial/pardoning<br>• Ordinance-making under Article 213<br><br>Governor's discretion<br><br>The Governor has discretion in certain constitutionally recognised situations, but the office is not an independent political executive.<br><br><br>C. JUDICIARY<br><br>India has an integrated judicial system.<br><br>Structure:<br><br>Supreme Court → High Courts → Subordinate Courts<br><br><br>Supreme Court<br><br>Article 124 establishes the Supreme Court.<br><br>Jurisdiction<br><br>1. Original jurisdiction<br><br>Article 131<br><br>Disputes involving:<br><br>• Union and States<br>• States inter se<br><br>where the dispute involves legal rights.<br><br>2. Writ jurisdiction<br><br>Article 32.<br><br>3. Appellate jurisdiction<br><br>Appeals involving:<br><br>• Constitutional matters<br>• Civil matters<br>• Criminal matters<br><br>4. Special Leave<br><br>Article 136.<br><br>Supreme Court can grant special leave to appeal from judgments/orders of courts and tribunals, subject to constitutional limitations.<br><br>5. Advisory jurisdiction<br><br>Article 143.<br><br>President may refer questions of law/fact of public importance to Supreme Court.<br><br>6. Review jurisdiction<br><br>Article 137.<br><br>Supreme Court can review its own judgments subject to constitutional/statutory rules.<br><br><br>High Courts<br><br>Article 214 provides for High Courts for States.<br><br>Article 226<br><br>High Courts can issue writs for:<br><br>• Fundamental Rights<br>• Other legal rights<br><br>This makes Article 226 wider in scope than Article 32.<br><br><br>Independence of Judiciary<br><br>Important safeguards include:<br><br>• Security of tenure<br>• Difficult removal procedure<br>• Salaries charged on Consolidated Fund<br>• Separation from executive<br>• Judicial review<br>• Contempt powers<br>• Institutional autonomy<br><br><br>Appointment of Judges<br><br>The Supreme Court's modern appointment system is based on the Collegium system, developed through the Judges Cases.<br><br>Three Judges Cases<br><br>1. S.P. Gupta v Union of India (1981) — First Judges Case<br>2. Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v Union of India (1993) — Second Judges Case<br>3. In re Presidential Reference (1998) — Third Judges Case<br><br>The NJAC was later introduced by constitutional amendment but struck down in:<br><br>Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v Union of India (2015)<br><br>The Supreme Court held that the NJAC framework violated the independence of judiciary, which forms part of the basic structure.<br><br><br>UNIT III — DISTRIBUTION OF POWERS BETWEEN UNION AND STATES<br><br>The Constitution divides legislative powers through:<br><br>• Union List<br>• State List<br>• Concurrent List<br><br>These are found in the Seventh Schedule.<br><br><br>A. Legislative Relations<br><br>Articles 245–255<br><br>Article 246<br><br>Distributes legislative subjects between:<br><br>• Parliament<br>• State Legislatures<br><br>Union List<br><br>Only Parliament ordinarily legislates.<br><br>Examples:<br><br>• Defence<br>• Foreign affairs<br>• Currency<br>• Railways<br>• Citizenship<br><br>State List<br><br>States ordinarily legislate.<br><br>Examples include:<br><br>• Police<br>• Public order<br>• Public health<br>• Agriculture<br>• Local government<br><br>Concurrent List<br><br>Both Union and States can legislate.<br><br>Examples:<br><br>• Criminal law<br>• Marriage<br>• Education<br>• Forests<br>• Labour-related matters<br><br><br>Residuary Powers<br><br>Article 248 gives Parliament power over matters not enumerated in the State or Concurrent Lists.<br><br>Thus, India gives residuary powers to the Union.<br><br><br>Parliament's Power Over State Subjects<br><br>Parliament can legislate on State List subjects in certain circumstances.<br><br>Article 249<br><br>Rajya Sabha may pass a resolution declaring that legislation by Parliament is necessary in the national interest.<br><br>Article 250<br><br>During National Emergency.<br><br>Article 252<br><br>Two or more States may request Parliament to legislate for them.<br><br>Article 253<br><br>Parliament can legislate to implement international treaties/agreements.<br><br>Article 356<br><br>During President's Rule, Parliament can exercise legislative powers relating to the State.<br><br><br>B. Administrative Relations<br><br>Articles 256–263.<br><br>The Constitution establishes mechanisms for administrative coordination.<br><br>Article 256<br><br>States must ensure compliance with parliamentary laws and may be subject to Union directions in constitutionally permitted circumstances.<br><br>Article 257<br><br>Union can issue directions to States in certain situations.<br><br>Inter-State Council<br><br>Article 263 allows establishment of an Inter-State Council.<br><br>Purpose:<br><br>• Coordination<br>• Discussion of disputes<br>• Policy cooperation<br><br><br>C. Financial Relations<br><br>Articles 268–293.<br><br>Important concepts:<br><br>• Distribution of taxes<br>• Grants-in-aid<br>• Finance Commission<br>• GST framework<br>• Borrowing powers<br><br>Finance Commission<br><br>Article 280.<br><br>It recommends:<br><br>• Distribution of tax revenues<br>• Principles of grants-in-aid<br>• Other financial matters referred to it<br><br><br>D. Important Constitutional Doctrines<br><br>1. Doctrine of Territorial Nexus<br><br>A State legislature normally legislates within its territorial sphere.<br><br>However, a law may have extra-territorial effects if there is a sufficient territorial nexus.<br><br>Two requirements<br><br>1. Nexus must be real.<br>2. Liability imposed must be connected with that nexus.<br><br>Case<br><br>State of Bombay v R.M.D. Chamarbaugwala<br><br>The Court accepted territorial nexus where sufficient connection existed between the State and the subject matter.<br><br><br>2. Doctrine of Pith and Substance<br><br>Used where legislation appears to overlap different legislative fields.<br><br>The Court looks at the true nature and character of the legislation.<br><br>The incidental encroachment into another legislative field does not necessarily invalidate the law.<br><br>Case<br><br>State of Bombay v F.N. Balsara<br><br>The doctrine was applied to determine the true nature of legislation.<br><br>Example<br><br>If a State law is primarily about a State subject but incidentally affects a Union subject, it may still be valid.<br><br><br>3. Doctrine of Colourable Legislation<br><br>Principle:<br><br>“What cannot be done directly cannot be done indirectly.”<br><br>The Court examines legislative competence.<br><br>If legislature constitutionally lacks power to enact a law, it cannot achieve the same result indirectly.<br><br>Case<br><br>K.C. Gajapati Narayan Deo v State of Orissa<br><br>The doctrine was explained as a limitation on legislative competence rather than merely legislative motive.<br><br><br>4. Doctrine of Repugnancy<br><br>Article 254 deals with repugnancy between Union and State legislation on Concurrent List subjects.<br><br>Where a State law conflicts with a valid Parliamentary law:<br><br>Parliamentary law generally prevails.<br><br>Exception<br><br>If State law has received Presidential assent, it may prevail within that State.<br><br>However, Parliament can subsequently override it.<br><br>Case<br><br>M. Karunanidhi v Union of India<br><br>The Supreme Court discussed the requirements for establishing repugnancy.<br><br><br>5. Harmonious Construction<br><br>Where two constitutional provisions or laws appear to conflict, courts attempt to interpret them so that both can operate harmoniously.<br><br>The objective is to avoid making one provision redundant.<br><br><br>UNIT IV — EMERGENCY, AMENDMENTS AND FREEDOM OF TRADE<br><br>A. Freedom of Trade, Commerce and Intercourse<br><br>Articles 301–307.<br><br>Article 301<br><br>Trade, commerce and intercourse throughout India shall be free, subject to constitutional restrictions.<br><br>The objective is to maintain economic unity of India.<br><br><br>Restrictions<br><br>Freedom under Article 301 is not absolute.<br><br>Article 302<br><br>Parliament can impose restrictions in public interest.<br><br>Article 303<br><br>Restrictions cannot ordinarily discriminate between States, subject to constitutional exceptions.<br><br>Article 304<br><br>States may impose certain taxes/restrictions subject to constitutional requirements.<br><br><br>Important Cases<br><br>Atiabari Tea Co. v State of Assam<br><br>The Supreme Court examined restrictions on movement/trade and the scope of Article 301.<br><br>Automobile Transport (Rajasthan) Ltd. v State of Rajasthan<br><br>The Court distinguished regulatory measures from restrictions that substantially impede free trade.<br><br>Jindal Stainless Ltd. v State of Haryana (2016)<br><br>A Constitution Bench substantially clarified the scope of Article 301 and taxation under the trade-and-commerce provisions.<br><br><br>B. Emergency Provisions<br><br>Emergency provisions are contained in Part XVIII.<br><br>Three types:<br><br>1. National Emergency — Article 352<br>2. President's Rule — Article 356<br>3. Financial Emergency — Article 360<br><br><br>1. National Emergency — Article 352<br><br>Can be proclaimed where security of India or any part thereof is threatened by:<br><br>• War<br>• External aggression<br>• Armed rebellion<br><br>Important change<br><br>The 44th Amendment replaced “internal disturbance” with armed rebellion.<br><br><br>Effects<br><br>Executive<br><br>Union executive power becomes significantly stronger.<br><br>Legislative<br><br>Parliament gets expanded legislative authority over State subjects.<br><br>Fundamental Rights<br><br>Article 358 concerns Article 19 during certain emergencies.<br><br>Article 359 permits suspension of the right to move courts for specified Fundamental Rights, subject to constitutional limitations.<br><br>After the 44th Amendment, Articles 20 and 21 cannot be suspended under Article 359.<br><br><br>2. President's Rule — Article 356<br><br>Applied where constitutional machinery of a State has failed.<br><br>President may assume certain functions of the State government and Parliament may exercise the legislative powers of the State legislature.<br><br>Important Case<br><br>S.R. Bommai v Union of India<br><br>Extremely important for exams.<br><br>The Supreme Court held:<br><br>• Article 356 is subject to judicial review.<br>• President's satisfaction is not completely immune.<br>• Federalism is an important constitutional principle.<br>• Majority should ordinarily be tested on the floor of the House.<br>• Article 356 cannot be used arbitrarily to destroy elected State governments.<br><br><br>3. Financial Emergency — Article 360<br><br>Can be proclaimed if financial stability or credit of India or any part thereof is threatened.<br><br>Important point:<br><br>Financial Emergency has never been proclaimed in India.<br><br><br>C. Amendment of Constitution<br><br>Article 368 deals with constitutional amendment.<br><br>The Constitution needs the ability to change with society, but amendment power is not unlimited.<br><br><br>Types of Amendments<br><br>1. Simple Majority<br><br>Certain provisions can be changed through ordinary legislative procedure.<br><br>2. Special Majority<br><br>Requires:<br><br>• Majority of total membership of each House<br>• Two-thirds of members present and voting<br><br>3. Special Majority + State Ratification<br><br>Required for certain federal provisions.<br><br>At least half of the State Legislatures must ratify.<br><br><br>Doctrine of Basic Structure<br><br>This is one of the most important topics in Constitutional Law.<br><br>Kesavananda Bharati v State of Kerala (1973)<br><br>The Supreme Court held:<br><br>“Parliament can amend the Constitution but cannot destroy its basic structure.”<br><br>Thus Article 368 gives a wide amendment power but not an unlimited power.<br><br><br>Elements of Basic Structure<br><br>The Court has recognised several features, including:<br><br>• Supremacy of Constitution<br>• Rule of law<br>• Judicial review<br>• Separation of powers<br>• Federalism<br>• Secularism<br>• Democracy<br>• Republican form of government<br>• Parliamentary system<br>• Free and fair elections<br>• Dignity and liberty<br>• Unity and integrity<br>• Limited amending power<br><br>The list is not exhaustive.<br><br><br>Important Basic Structure Cases<br><br>Golaknath v State of Punjab (1967)<br><br>Held that Parliament could not amend Fundamental Rights.<br><br>This position was later modified.<br><br>Kesavananda Bharati (1973)<br><br>Established basic structure doctrine.<br><br>Indira Gandhi v Raj Narain (1975)<br><br>Free and fair elections recognised as part of basic structure.<br><br>Minerva Mills v Union of India (1980)<br><br>Held that:<br><br>• Limited amending power itself is part of basic structure.<br>• Balance between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles is important.<br><br>I.R. Coelho v State of Tamil Nadu (2007)<br><br>Laws inserted into Ninth Schedule after 24 April 1973 can be reviewed if they damage basic structure.<br><br><br>CONSTITUTIONAL LAW – II<br><br>UNIT I — FUNDAMENTAL RIGHTS – I<br><br>Fundamental Rights are contained in Part III, Articles 12–35.<br><br>They are enforceable by courts.<br><br><br>1. Evolution of Fundamental Rights<br><br>The idea of fundamental rights developed from:<br><br>• Colonial experience<br>• Freedom movement<br>• Nehru Report<br>• Constituent Assembly debates<br>• Liberal democratic principles<br><br>Their primary purpose is to protect individual liberty against arbitrary State action.<br><br><br>2. Article 12 — Definition of State<br><br>Article 12 defines "State" for Part III.<br><br>It includes:<br><br>• Government of India<br>• Parliament<br>• State Governments<br>• State Legislatures<br>• Local authorities<br>• Other authorities within India or under control of Government of India<br><br><br>Instrumentality/Agency Doctrine<br><br>Courts have expanded Article 12 to cover bodies that function as instruments or agencies of government.<br><br>Important cases<br><br>R.D. Shetty v International Airport Authority<br><br>Ajay Hasia v Khalid Mujib<br><br>The Court developed tests for determining whether a body is an instrumentality or agency of government.<br><br>Important point<br><br>A body does not escape Article 12 merely because it is formally incorporated as a separate legal entity.<br><br><br>3. Article 13<br><br>Article 13 establishes constitutional supremacy over laws inconsistent with Fundamental Rights.<br><br>It provides the foundation for judicial review.<br><br><br>Doctrine of Severability<br><br>If only a part of legislation is unconstitutional, the unconstitutional portion may be separated if the remaining portion can operate independently.<br><br><br>Doctrine of Eclipse<br><br>A pre-Constitution law inconsistent with Fundamental Rights becomes unenforceable to the extent of inconsistency.<br><br>It is not necessarily treated as completely dead for all purposes.<br><br><br>Waiver of Fundamental Rights<br><br>Generally, an individual cannot waive Fundamental Rights merely because they voluntarily agree to give them up.<br><br><br>UNIT I — ARTICLE 14<br><br>Equality Before Law<br><br>Article 14 provides:<br><br>• Equality before law<br>• Equal protection of laws<br><br><br>Equality before law<br><br>Negative concept.<br><br>No person is above law.<br><br><br>Equal protection<br><br>Positive concept.<br><br>People similarly situated should receive similar treatment.<br><br><br>Reasonable Classification<br><br>Article 14 does not require identical treatment of everyone.<br><br>Classification is valid when:<br><br>1. Intelligible Differentia<br><br>There must be a clear basis distinguishing the grouped persons from others.<br><br>2. Rational Nexus<br><br>The differentia must have a rational connection with the objective of the law.<br><br><br>Modern Article 14 Doctrine<br><br>Article 14 also prohibits arbitrariness.<br><br>E.P. Royappa v State of Tamil Nadu<br><br>The Court moved away from a narrow classification approach and connected equality with non-arbitrariness.<br><br>Maneka Gandhi v Union of India<br><br>Article 14 was interpreted broadly along with Articles 19 and 21.<br><br>Shayara Bano v Union of India<br><br>The doctrine of manifest arbitrariness was discussed in the context of constitutional review.<br><br><br>Legitimate Expectation<br><br>A person may have a legitimate expectation that a public authority will act consistently with its established representation or past practice, subject to law and public interest.<br><br>It does not automatically create an enforceable right.<br><br><br>Compensatory Discrimination<br><br>Constitution permits certain affirmative measures to address historical and social disadvantage.<br><br>Examples:<br><br>• Article 15(3)<br>• Article 15(4)<br>• Article 15(5)<br>• Article 15(6)<br>• Article 16(4)<br>• Article 16(4A)<br><br>The idea is substantive equality, rather than merely formal equality.<br><br><br>ARTICLE 19 — FREEDOMS<br><br>Article 19 currently guarantees six major freedoms to citizens:<br><br>1. Speech and expression<br>2. Peaceful assembly without arms<br>3. Association/unions/co-operative societies<br>4. Movement throughout India<br>5. Residence and settlement<br>6. Profession, occupation, trade or business<br><br><br>Freedom of Speech and Expression<br><br>Article 19(1)(a).<br><br>Includes:<br><br>• Freedom of press<br>• Political expression<br>• Right to receive information<br>• Artistic expression<br>• Online expression<br>• Criticism of government<br><br>But it is subject to reasonable restrictions under Article 19(2).<br><br>Grounds include:<br><br>• Sovereignty and integrity of India<br>• Security of State<br>• Friendly relations with foreign States<br>• Public order<br>• Decency or morality<br>• Contempt of court<br>• Defamation<br>• Incitement to an offence<br><br><br>Important Cases<br><br>Romesh Thappar v State of Madras<br><br>Freedom of political expression is fundamental to democracy.<br><br>Bennett Coleman &amp; Co. v Union of India<br><br>Freedom of press is protected under Article 19(1)(a).<br><br>Shreya Singhal v Union of India<br><br>Section 66A of the IT Act was struck down for violating freedom of speech.<br><br><br>Reasonable Restrictions<br><br>Restrictions must:<br><br>• Be imposed by law<br>• Fall within constitutional grounds<br>• Have rational connection<br>• Be reasonable<br>• Not be arbitrary or excessive<br><br><br>UNIT II — FUNDAMENTAL RIGHTS II<br><br>ARTICLE 21<br><br>Article 21:<br><br>No person shall be deprived of life or personal liberty except according to procedure established by law.<br><br>Originally interpreted narrowly.<br><br>A.K. Gopalan v State of Madras<br><br>Initially, fundamental rights were treated relatively independently.<br><br><br>Maneka Gandhi v Union of India<br><br>This radically expanded Article 21.<br><br>The procedure under Article 21 must be:<br><br>• Fair<br>• Just<br>• Reasonable<br><br>Articles 14, 19 and 21 are now often described as forming a constitutional relationship.<br><br><br>Rights Included Under Article 21<br><br>Judicial interpretation has expanded Article 21 to include various dimensions of dignified life.<br><br>Examples:<br><br>• Right to privacy<br>• Right to livelihood<br>• Right to shelter<br>• Right to clean environment<br>• Right to legal aid<br>• Right to speedy trial<br>• Right to dignity<br>• Right against custodial violence<br><br><br>Important Cases<br><br>Francis Coralie Mullin v Administrator<br><br>Life means more than mere animal existence.<br><br>Olga Tellis v Bombay Municipal Corporation<br><br>Right to livelihood was connected with Article 21.<br><br>D.K. Basu v State of West Bengal<br><br>Guidelines concerning custodial arrest/detention and protection against custodial abuse.<br><br>Justice K.S. Puttaswamy v Union of India<br><br>Right to privacy recognised as a fundamental right.<br><br><br>Article 21A — Right to Education<br><br>Inserted by the 86th Amendment, 2002.<br><br>Provides free and compulsory education for children in the constitutionally specified age group.<br><br>RTE Act, 2009<br><br>The Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act operationalises Article 21A.<br><br>Cases<br><br>Mohini Jain v State of Karnataka<br><br>Unni Krishnan v State of Andhra Pradesh<br><br>These cases contributed significantly to constitutional recognition of educational rights.<br><br><br>Article 22<br><br>Provides safeguards against:<br><br>• Arbitrary arrest<br>• Detention<br><br>Important safeguards include:<br><br>• Right to be informed of grounds of arrest<br>• Right to consult and be defended by legal practitioner<br>• Production before magistrate within prescribed period<br><br>However, preventive detention is constitutionally recognised subject to safeguards.<br><br><br>ARTICLE 23 — TRAFFICKING AND FORCED LABOUR<br><br>Article 23 prohibits:<br><br>• Human trafficking<br>• Begar<br>• Forced labour<br><br>Applies against both State and private actors.<br><br>People's Union for Democratic Rights v Union of India<br><br>Payment below minimum wages in certain circumstances may amount to forced labour.<br><br>Bandhua Mukti Morcha v Union of India<br><br>The Supreme Court strengthened protection against bonded labour.<br><br><br>ARTICLE 24 — CHILD LABOUR<br><br>No child below the constitutionally specified age may be employed in a factory, mine or hazardous employment.<br><br>It reflects the constitutional protection of children against exploitation.<br><br><br>ARTICLE 25 — FREEDOM OF RELIGION<br><br>Every person is entitled to:<br><br>• Freedom of conscience<br>• Profess religion<br>• Practise religion<br>• Propagate religion<br><br>Subject to:<br><br>• Public order<br>• Morality<br>• Health<br>• Other Fundamental Rights<br><br><br>Essential Religious Practices Doctrine<br><br>Commissioner, Hindu Religious Endowments v Sri Lakshmindra Thirtha Swamiar<br><br>Known as the Shirur Mutt case.<br><br>The Court developed the idea of determining what constitutes an essential religious practice.<br><br><br>Article 26<br><br>Religious denominations can:<br><br>• Establish institutions<br>• Manage religious affairs<br>• Own property<br>• Administer property according to law<br><br><br>Article 27<br><br>No person can be compelled to pay a tax specifically appropriated for promotion/maintenance of a particular religion.<br><br><br>Article 28<br><br>Deals with religious instruction in educational institutions.<br><br><br>Articles 29–30 — Minority Rights<br><br>Article 29<br><br>Protects:<br><br>• Language<br>• Script<br>• Culture<br><br>Article 30<br><br>Minorities have the right to establish and administer educational institutions.<br><br><br>Important Cases<br><br>St. Stephen's College v University of Delhi<br><br>T.M.A. Pai Foundation v State of Karnataka<br><br>P.A. Inamdar v State of Maharashtra<br><br>These cases deal extensively with minority educational rights and State regulation.<br><br><br>UNIT III — RIGHT TO CONSTITUTIONAL REMEDIES<br><br>This is one of the most important units.<br><br>Article 32<br><br>Dr. B.R. Ambedkar described Article 32 as the “heart and soul” of the Constitution.<br><br>It allows individuals to approach the Supreme Court for enforcement of Fundamental Rights.<br><br><br>Article 226<br><br>High Courts can issue writs for:<br><br>• Fundamental Rights<br>• Other legal rights<br><br>Therefore Article 226 is broader in scope than Article 32.<br><br><br>Five Constitutional Writs<br><br>1. Habeas Corpus<br><br>Meaning:<br><br>“You may have the body.”<br><br>Used to challenge unlawful detention.<br><br>Purpose:<br><br>• Protect personal liberty<br>• Secure release from illegal detention<br><br><br>2. Mandamus<br><br>Meaning:<br><br>Command.<br><br>Issued to compel a public authority to perform a legal/public duty.<br><br>Generally not issued to:<br><br>• Enforce purely private obligations<br>• Control purely discretionary matters where no legal duty exists<br><br><br>3. Certiorari<br><br>Used by a superior court to quash an order of a lower court/tribunal where there is:<br><br>• Jurisdictional error<br>• Error of law<br>• Violation of natural justice<br><br>Generally corrective in nature.<br><br><br>4. Prohibition<br><br>Issued to prevent a lower court/tribunal from continuing proceedings beyond its jurisdiction.<br><br>Difference<br><br>Prohibition → prevents<br><br>Certiorari → quashes<br><br><br>5. Quo Warranto<br><br>Meaning:<br><br>“By what authority?”<br><br>Used to challenge illegal occupation of a public office.<br><br>The applicant need not necessarily show personal injury in the same manner as ordinary litigation.<br><br><br>Public Interest Litigation<br><br>PIL allows litigation for protection of public interest, particularly where affected persons cannot effectively approach courts themselves.<br><br>Important Cases<br><br>S.P. Gupta v Union of India<br><br>Bandhua Mukti Morcha v Union of India<br><br>People's Union for Democratic Rights v Union of India<br><br>PIL is important for:<br><br>• Bonded labour<br>• Prisoners' rights<br>• Environmental protection<br>• Child labour<br>• Rights of vulnerable communities<br>• Government accountability<br><br><br>UNIT IV — DIRECTIVE PRINCIPLES AND FUNDAMENTAL DUTIES<br><br>Directive Principles of State Policy<br><br>Articles 36–51.<br><br>They are contained in Part IV.<br><br>Article 37<br><br>DPSPs:<br><br>• Are not enforceable by courts<br>• Are nevertheless fundamental in governance<br>• Should guide the State in making laws and policies<br><br><br>Classification of DPSPs<br><br>Socialist Principles<br><br>Examples:<br><br>• Article 38 — social order<br>• Article 39 — adequate livelihood, distribution of resources<br>• Article 39A — free legal aid<br>• Article 41 — right to work/education/public assistance<br>• Article 42 — humane conditions of work<br>• Article 43 — living wage<br><br><br>Gandhian Principles<br><br>Examples:<br><br>• Article 40 — village panchayats<br>• Article 43 — cottage industries<br>• Article 46 — weaker sections<br>• Article 47 — public health<br>• Article 48 — agriculture and animal husbandry<br><br><br>Liberal-Intellectual Principles<br><br>Examples:<br><br>• Article 44 — Uniform Civil Code<br>• Article 45 — early childhood care/education<br>• Article 48A — environment<br>• Article 49 — monuments<br>• Article 50 — separation of judiciary from executive<br>• Article 51 — international peace<br><br><br>Fundamental Rights vs DPSP<br><br>Initially there was tension between them.<br><br>State of Madras v Champakam Dorairajan (1951)<br><br>The Supreme Court held that Fundamental Rights prevailed in case of conflict.<br><br>But constitutional amendments and later judicial interpretation developed a more balanced approach.<br><br><br>Kesavananda Bharati<br><br>The Court recognised the importance of both:<br><br>• Fundamental Rights<br>• Directive Principles<br><br><br>Minerva Mills v Union of India<br><br>Extremely important.<br><br>The Supreme Court held that harmony and balance between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles is part of the basic structure.<br><br>Neither Part III nor Part IV can be completely destroyed in favour of the other.<br><br><br>FUNDAMENTAL DUTIES<br><br>Article 51A contains Fundamental Duties.<br><br>They were introduced through the 42nd Constitutional Amendment, 1976.<br><br>An additional duty concerning education of children was added by the 86th Amendment.<br><br><br>Important Fundamental Duties<br><br>Citizens should:<br><br>• Respect Constitution<br>• Respect national symbols<br>• Cherish ideals of freedom struggle<br>• Protect sovereignty and integrity<br>• Promote harmony<br>• Renounce practices derogatory to dignity of women<br>• Preserve cultural heritage<br>• Protect environment<br>• Develop scientific temper<br>• Safeguard public property<br>• Avoid violence<br>• Strive for excellence<br>• Provide educational opportunities to children in the constitutionally specified age group<br><br><br>Legal Importance of Fundamental Duties<br><br>Although generally not directly enforceable like Fundamental Rights, they can assist courts in:<br><br>• Constitutional interpretation<br>• Determining validity of legislation<br>• Promoting civic responsibility<br>• Understanding legislative objectives<br><br>Important case<br><br>M.C. Mehta v Union of India<br><br>The Supreme Court used constitutional environmental obligations and duties to strengthen environmental protection.<br><br><br>MOST IMPORTANT CASES TO MEMORISE<br><br>For your exam, I would especially prioritise these:<br><br>Preamble — Kesavananda Bharati<br><br>Basic Structure — Kesavananda Bharati<br><br>Basic Structure — Minerva Mills<br><br>Federalism — S.R. Bommai<br><br>Parliamentary Privilege — Raja Ram Pal<br><br>Judicial Independence — NJAC Case (2015)<br><br>Article 14 — E.P. Royappa<br><br>Article 14 — Maneka Gandhi<br><br>Article 19 — Bennett Coleman<br><br>Article 19 — Shreya Singhal<br><br>Article 21 — Maneka Gandhi<br><br>Privacy — K.S. Puttaswamy<br><br>Livelihood — Olga Tellis<br><br>Custodial Rights — D.K. Basu<br><br>Forced Labour — PUDR<br><br>Bonded Labour — Bandhua Mukti Morcha<br><br>Religious Freedom — Shirur Mutt<br><br>Minority Education — T.M.A. Pai<br><br>Article 32 — S.P. Gupta / Bandhua Mukti Morcha<br><br>DPSP — Champakam Dorairajan<br><br>FR + DPSP — Minerva Mills<br><br>Territorial Nexus — R.M.D. Chamarbaugwala<br><br>Pith &amp; Substance — F.N. Balsara<br><br>Colourable Legislation — K.C. Gajapati Narayan Deo<br><br>Repugnancy — M. Karunanidhi<br><br>Emergency — S.R. Bommai<br><br>Emergency &amp; Liberty — ADM Jabalpur<br><br>Trade &amp; Commerce — Atiabari Tea<br><br>Trade &amp; Commerce — Automobile Transport<br></div>
