"Constitutional Law I": `
<h4>UNIT I — Meaning and Concept of Constitution and Constitutionalism</h4>
<p><b>Constitution:</b> The fundamental and supreme law of a country establishing the structure of government, governmental powers, distribution of powers, rights and limitations on governmental authority.</p>
<p>The Indian Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949 and came into force on 26 January 1950.</p>
<h4>Constitutionalism</h4>
<p>Constitutionalism means that governmental power must be limited and exercised according to constitutional rules.</p>
<h4>UNIT II — Constitutional Organs of Government</h4>
<p>Parliament consists of the President, Rajya Sabha and Lok Sabha under Article 79. The Executive includes the President and the Council of Ministers. India has an integrated judicial system consisting of the Supreme Court, High Courts and subordinate courts.</p>
<h4>UNIT III — Distribution of Powers Between Union and States</h4>
<p>Legislative powers are distributed through the Union List, State List and Concurrent List in the Seventh Schedule. Articles 245–255 deal with legislative relations. Article 248 gives residuary legislative powers to Parliament.</p>
<p>Important doctrines: Territorial Nexus, Pith and Substance, Colourable Legislation, Repugnancy and Harmonious Construction.</p>
<h4>UNIT IV — Emergency, Amendments and Freedom of Trade</h4>
<p>Emergency provisions are contained in Part XVIII: National Emergency under Article 352, President's Rule under Article 356 and Financial Emergency under Article 360.</p>
<p>Article 368 deals with constitutional amendment. Kesavananda Bharati v State of Kerala (1973) established the Basic Structure Doctrine.</p>
