<h4>UNIT I — Fundamental Rights I</h4>
<p>Fundamental Rights are contained in Part III, Articles 12–35. Article 12 defines State and Article 13 establishes constitutional supremacy over laws inconsistent with Fundamental Rights.</p>
<p>Article 14 guarantees equality before law and equal protection of laws. Article 19 guarantees six major freedoms to citizens, subject to reasonable restrictions. Article 21 protects life and personal liberty.</p>
<h4>UNIT II — Fundamental Rights II</h4>
<p>Article 21 has been broadly interpreted to include dignity, privacy, livelihood, legal aid, speedy trial and other aspects of dignified life. Article 25 protects freedom of conscience and religion, subject to constitutional limitations.</p>
<p>Articles 29–30 protect cultural and educational rights, including minority educational institutions.</p>
<h4>UNIT III — Constitutional Remedies</h4>
<p>Article 32 permits approaching the Supreme Court for enforcement of Fundamental Rights. Article 226 gives High Courts power to issue writs for Fundamental Rights and other legal rights.</p>
<p>The five writs are Habeas Corpus, Mandamus, Certiorari, Prohibition and Quo Warranto.</p>
<h4>UNIT IV — DPSPs and Fundamental Duties</h4>
<p>Directive Principles are contained in Articles 36–51. Article 37 states that they are not enforceable by courts but are fundamental in governance.</p>
<p>Article 51A contains Fundamental Duties. They were introduced by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment, 1976, with an additional education-related duty inserted by the 86th Amendment.</p>
