<h4>Constitution — Meaning</h4>
<p>A Constitution establishes government, distributes powers, limits governmental authority, protects individual liberty, establishes rule of law and provides mechanisms for constitutional change.</p>
<h4>Classification</h4>
<p>Constitutions may be written or unwritten, rigid or flexible, and federal or unitary. The Indian Constitution is written, partly rigid and partly flexible, and federal in structure with strong unitary features.</p>
<h4>Preamble</h4>
<p>The Preamble begins with “We, the People of India” and declares India to be Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic and Republic. It promises Justice, Liberty, Equality and Fraternity.</p>
<p>Kesavananda Bharati v State of Kerala (1973) held that the Preamble is part of the Constitution and can assist constitutional interpretation.</p>
