"Constitutional Organs": `
<h4>Parliament</h4>
<p>Article 79 provides that Parliament consists of the President, Rajya Sabha and Lok Sabha. Lok Sabha is directly elected and normally has a five-year term. Rajya Sabha is a permanent House.</p>
<h4>Executive</h4>
<p>The President is the constitutional head of the Union Executive. Under Article 74, the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers. The Governor is the constitutional head of a State.</p>
<h4>Judiciary</h4>
<p>Article 124 establishes the Supreme Court. Article 32 concerns writ jurisdiction; Article 131 original jurisdiction; Article 136 special leave; Article 143 advisory jurisdiction and Article 137 review.</p>
<p>Article 226 gives High Courts wider writ jurisdiction. Judicial independence is a basic constitutional principle.</p>
`,
"Union–State Relations": `
<h4>Legislative Relations</h4>
<p>Articles 245–255 and Article 246 distribute legislative powers. The Seventh Schedule contains the Union, State and Concurrent Lists.</p>
<h4>Important Doctrines</h4>
<p><b>Territorial Nexus:</b> A State law may have extra-territorial effects where sufficient territorial nexus exists.</p>
<p><b>Pith and Substance:</b> Courts examine the true nature and character of legislation.</p>
<p><b>Colourable Legislation:</b> What cannot be done directly cannot be done indirectly.</p>
<p><b>Repugnancy:</b> Article 254 deals with conflict between Parliamentary and State legislation on Concurrent List matters.</p>
<p><b>Harmonious Construction:</b> Conflicting provisions should, where possible, be interpreted so that both can operate.</p>
`,
"Emergency & Amendment": `
<h4>National Emergency — Article 352</h4>
<p>A National Emergency may be proclaimed where the security of India or any part thereof is threatened by war, external aggression or armed rebellion.</p>
<h4>President's Rule — Article 356</h4>
<p>It may be applied where constitutional machinery of a State has failed. S.R. Bommai v Union of India (1994) established important limits on the use of Article 356 and recognised federalism as an important constitutional principle.</p>
<h4>Financial Emergency — Article 360</h4>
<p>It may be proclaimed where the financial stability or credit of India or any part thereof is threatened. It has never been proclaimed in India.</p>
<h4>Basic Structure</h4>
<p>Kesavananda Bharati v State of Kerala (1973) held that Parliament can amend the Constitution but cannot destroy its basic structure. Minerva Mills v Union of India (1980) emphasised limited amending power and harmony between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles.</p>
`,
"Fundamental Rights": `
<h4>Article 12</h4>
<p>State includes the Government and Parliament of India, State Governments and Legislatures, local authorities and other authorities within the constitutional definition.</p>
<h4>Article 14</h4>
<p>Guarantees equality before law and equal protection of laws. E.P. Royappa and Maneka Gandhi developed the modern understanding of equality and non-arbitrariness.</p>
<h4>Article 19</h4>
<p>Protects speech and expression, peaceful assembly, association, movement, residence and settlement, and profession, occupation, trade or business, subject to constitutional restrictions.</p>
<h4>Article 21</h4>
<p>Protects life and personal liberty. Maneka Gandhi expanded the meaning of fair, just and reasonable procedure. Puttaswamy recognised privacy as a Fundamental Right.</p>
`,
"Constitutional Remedies & PIL": `
<h4>Article 32</h4>
<p>Article 32 enables individuals to approach the Supreme Court for enforcement of Fundamental Rights and was described by Dr. B.R. Ambedkar as the “heart and soul” of the Constitution.</p>
<h4>Article 226</h4>
<p>High Courts may issue writs for Fundamental Rights and other legal rights.</p>
<h4>Five Writs</h4>
<p>Habeas Corpus — challenges unlawful detention.</p>
<p>Mandamus — commands performance of a legal/public duty.</p>
<p>Certiorari — may quash an order of a lower court or tribunal.</p>
<p>Prohibition — prevents a lower court or tribunal from continuing proceedings beyond jurisdiction.</p>
<p>Quo Warranto — challenges unlawful occupation of a public office.</p>
<h4>PIL</h4>
<p>Public Interest Litigation facilitates judicial protection of public interests, particularly where affected persons cannot effectively approach courts themselves.</p>
`,
"DPSPs & Fundamental Duties": `
<h4>Directive Principles</h4>
<p>Articles 36–51 are contained in Part IV. Under Article 37, DPSPs are not enforceable by courts but are fundamental in governance.</p>
<p>They include socialist, Gandhian and liberal-intellectual principles such as social justice, village panchayats, free legal aid, public health, environmental protection and international peace.</p>
<h4>Fundamental Duties</h4>
<p>Article 51A contains Fundamental Duties. They include respecting the Constitution, protecting sovereignty and integrity, promoting harmony, protecting the environment, developing scientific temper, safeguarding public property and striving for excellence.</p>
<p>Minerva Mills v Union of India recognised the importance of harmony and balance between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles.</p>
`,
"Important Cases": `
<h4>High-Priority Cases</h4>
<p>Kesavananda Bharati v State of Kerala — Basic Structure Doctrine and Preamble.</p>
<p>S.R. Bommai v Union of India — Federalism and Article 356.</p>
<p>E.P. Royappa v State of Tamil Nadu — Equality and arbitrariness.</p>
<p>Maneka Gandhi v Union of India — Articles 14, 19 and 21.</p>
<p>Bennett Coleman & Co. v Union of India — Freedom of press.</p>
<p>Shreya Singhal v Union of India — Freedom of speech and Section 66A.</p>
<p>Justice K.S. Puttaswamy v Union of India — Right to privacy.</p>
<p>Olga Tellis v Bombay Municipal Corporation — Right to livelihood.</p>
<p>D.K. Basu v State of West Bengal — Custodial safeguards.</p>
<p>Bandhua Mukti Morcha — Bonded labour.</p>
<p>Shirur Mutt — Essential Religious Practices.</p>
<p>T.M.A. Pai Foundation — Minority educational rights.</p>
<p>State of Bombay v R.M.D. Chamarbaugwala — Territorial Nexus.</p>
<p>State of Bombay v F.N. Balsara — Pith and Substance.</p>
<p>K.C. Gajapati Narayan Deo v State of Orissa — Colourable Legislation.</p>
<p>M. Karunanidhi v Union of India — Repugnancy.</p>
<p>Atiabari Tea Co. v State of Assam and Automobile Transport (Rajasthan) Ltd. v State of Rajasthan — Freedom of Trade and Commerce.</p>
`
};

function openModal(i){
  const title=items[i][1];
  if(title==="Constitutional Law I" || title==="Constitutional Law II"){
    document.getElementById("grid").style.display="none";
    document.querySelector(".heading").style.display="none";
    document.getElementById("constitutional-notes").classList.add("show");
    document.getElementById("constitutional-notes").scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth"});
    return;
  }
  mt.textContent=title;
  if(constitutionalContent[title]) mp.innerHTML=constitutionalContent[title];
  else mp.textContent=items[i][3];
  modal.classList.add("show");
}
function closeModal(){modal.classList.remove("show")}
function closeNotes(){
  document.getElementById("constitutional-notes").classList.remove("show");
  document.getElementById("grid").style.display="";
  document.querySelector(".heading").style.display="";
  document.getElementById("library").scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth"});
}

search.oninput=e=>render(e.target.value);modal.onclick=e=>{if(e.target===modal)closeModal()};render();
